#N canvas 40 60 1280 860 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 01\ \ \ McCulloch-Pitts\ neuron\ (1943)\ \ -\ \ logic\ with\ rhythms 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 830 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 830 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 830 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 The very first artificial neuron. It cannot learn and it only knows 0 and 1 - but with the right weights it makes decisions. Here its three inputs are rhythms and every time it fires it plays a bell: a drum machine that thinks in logic., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 470 empty empty THE\ IDEA 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 In 1943 the neurophysiologist Warren McCulloch and the 19-year-old logician Walter Pitts described a neuron as a tiny logic machine., f 52;
#X text 20 173 Every input x is 0 (silent) or 1 (active). Every input has a weight w: +1 excites \, -1 inhibits. The neuron adds up w * x and FIRES (outputs 1) only if the sum reaches its threshold:, f 52;
#X text 30 239 y = 1 if w1*x1 + w2*x2 + w3*x3 >= threshold \, otherwise y = 0, f 52;
#X text 20 275 Choose weights and threshold and one neuron becomes AND \, OR \, NOT ... McCulloch and Pitts proved that networks of such units can compute anything a logic circuit can - John von Neumann borrowed the idea for the design of the first stored-program computers (1945)., f 52;
#X text 20 371 There is no learning yet: YOU are the learning algorithm. Pick weights with the presets or type your own numbers (click a number box \, type \, press Enter - or drag it)., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 520 470 empty empty PLAY:\ three\ rhythms\ in\,\ one\ decision\ out 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
