#X text 960 360 VOLUME: drag to start - it also turns DSP on. Start low!, f 40;
#X text 960 420 Bell = the neuron fired. Clicks = the inputs (turn them down to hear only the decision)., f 40;
#X obj 10 576 cnv 1 1260 240 empty empty TRY\ THIS 8 12 0 13 #e6f6e9 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 602 1) Press play \, raise the volume. Start with AND: the bell rings only when A and B hit together (every 12 steps)., f 86;
#X text 20 638 2) OR \, then A AND NOT B: an inhibitory weight (-1) cancels A whenever B plays. Then NOR: the neuron plays only in the gaps the others leave., f 86;
#X text 20 674 3) Invent rhythms: set w3 = 2 and threshold 2 - C alone is now enough to fire. Change [mod 5] into [mod 7] (edit mode \, double-click) for 7-against-4., f 86;
#X text 20 710 4) Try to make the bell ring when EXACTLY ONE of A or B plays (XOR)... you will fail \, and that is a famous result (see lessons 4 and 5)., f 86;
#X text 640 602 MUSIC LINK: logic modules (AND / OR / XOR of gate signals) are standard tools in modular synths and generative sequencers. A McCulloch-Pitts neuron is a programmable logic module: its weights ARE the patch., f 88;
#X text 640 653 IN PD: the sum is one [expr] with 6 inlets - A \, B \, C on the left \, the weights on the right. [t f f f] fires right-to-left \, so C and B arrive (cold inlets) before A triggers the calculation (hot inlet)., f 88;
